    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-tell"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-tell.jpg" alt="William Tell" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landesmuseum_Z%C3%BCrich_2010-09-20_14-38-14_ShiftN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans Sandreuter; photograph: Roland zh, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederacy grew out of the rural valley communities around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-lucerne">Lake Lucerne</a>. The opening of a route over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard Pass</a> in the early thirteenth century, made possible by bridges through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schollenen-gorge">Schöllenen</a> gorge, gave the valleys of Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden new strategic and commercial value, and Uri in 1231 and Schwyz in 1240 obtained charters placing them directly under the emperor rather than under regional lords. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, who held extensive rights in the region, sought to consolidate their power, the communities drew together. The Federal Charter, dated to early August 1291 and probably renewing an older agreement, bound Uri, Schwyz and Unterwalden to help each other against outside attack and to settle disputes among themselves by arbitration. The legends of the Rütli oath and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-tell">William Tell</a>, written down around 1470, gave the alliance a heroic origin, while the Charter itself was adopted as the founding document only in the nineteenth century. After the three communities defeated a Habsburg army under Duke Leopold I at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten">Battle of Morgarten</a> in 1315, they renewed their alliance at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-brunnen">Brunnen</a>. Cities and valleys then joined through separate treaties: Lucerne in 1332, Zürich in 1351, Glarus and Zug in 1352 and Bern in 1353, forming the league of 'eight places' that dominated the region by the late fourteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/geneva"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/geneva.jpg" alt="Geneva" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geneve_2005_001_Ork.ch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">fr:Utilisateur:Stéphane_Pecorini, CC BY-SA 2.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederacy combined two different worlds. In the Alpine cantons of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, peasant communities lived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and dairy farming, moving herds to high summer pastures and exporting cattle, cheese and butter southwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, while traffic over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-pass">Gotthard</a> brought tolls and carrying trade. The city cantons of the plateau, among them Zürich, Bern, Basel, Lucerne and Fribourg, were market towns ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> or patrician families, with rural territories of their own. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">Mercenary</a> service was a major source of income: thousands of young men served foreign princes, and the cantons and their leading families received pensions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and others in return for recruiting rights. From the sixteenth century textiles gained in importance, with linen around St Gallen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> in Zürich and Basel, while Protestant refugees brought skills such as watchmaking to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and the Jura. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> divided society: Zürich, Bern, Basel and Schaffhausen became Reformed, the central cantons together with Fribourg and Solothurn stayed Catholic, and Glarus and Appenzell were mixed or split. Basel, with its university founded in 1460 and printers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-froben">Johann Froben</a>, became a centre of humanism where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> lived and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-holbein-the-younger">Hans Holbein the Younger</a> worked, and in the eighteenth century the Bernoulli family, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonhard-euler">Leonhard Euler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-haller">Albrecht von Haller</a> gave the Confederacy a leading place in European science.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten.jpg" alt="Battle of Morgarten" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten">Battle of Morgarten</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bendicht_Tschachtlan,_Die_Schlacht_am_Morgarten_(c._1470).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bendicht Tschachtlan., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederates owed their survival and reputation to their infantry. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morgarten">Battle of Morgarten</a> in 1315 they ambushed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army between a mountain slope and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ageri">Lake Ägeri</a>; at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> in 1386 they defeated and killed Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III of Austria</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nafels">Battle of Näfels</a> in 1388 the men of Glarus routed another Habsburg force. Massed columns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> and halberdiers became the most feared infantry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundian-wars">Burgundian Wars</a> they defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grandson">Battle of Grandson</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-morat">Battle of Murten</a> in 1476 and, as allies of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-ii-duke-of-lorraine">René II of Lorraine</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a> in 1477, where Charles was killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-war">Swabian War</a> of 1499 against Maximilian I and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> ended in a Swiss victory. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> the Confederates briefly controlled the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-novara-1513">Battle of Novara</a> in 1513, but their defeat by Francis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marignano">Battle of Marignano</a> in 1515 ended their ambitions as a great power. Thereafter Swiss soldiers fought chiefly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, above all for France, and from 1506 as the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontifical-swiss-guard">Swiss Guard</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-kappel">Kappel Wars</a> of 1529 and 1531, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villmergen-wars">Villmergen Wars</a> of 1656 and 1712 and the crushing of the Peasants' War of 1653 showed that the cantons' arms could also be turned inwards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/niklaus-von-flue"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niklaus-von-flue.jpg" alt="Niklaus von Flüe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/niklaus-von-flue">Niklaus von Flüe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicholas-Flue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">User Geogre on en.wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederacy never had a central government, a capital, a common treasury or a standing army. It was a network of alliances in which each member, called an <em>Ort</em> or canton, remained sovereign. The rural cantons were governed by open-air assemblies of adult men, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a></em>, while the cities were ruled by councils dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, as in Zürich and Basel, or by narrowing circles of patrician families, as in Bern, Lucerne, Fribourg and Solothurn. Common affairs were handled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> (<em>Tagsatzung</em>), a meeting of envoys who were bound by instructions and could decide only by agreement. A few common compacts provided shared rules: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pfaffenbrief">Priests' Charter</a> (<em>Pfaffenbrief</em>) of 1370, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sempach-charter">Sempach Charter</a> of 1393 on conduct in war, and the Stans Agreement of 1481, reached with the help of the hermit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklaus-von-flue">Niklaus von Flüe</a>, which admitted Fribourg and Solothurn and set out procedures for keeping the internal peace. The cantons jointly ruled conquered territories such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1415, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgau">Thurgau</a>, taken in 1460, and the Italian-speaking bailiwicks south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, sending bailiffs in rotation. Allied 'associates' such as the Abbey and City of St Gallen, the Three Leagues, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, Mulhouse and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> stood around this core. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, Catholic and Reformed cantons often met separately, and religious division frequently paralysed common action.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli.jpg" alt="Huldrych Zwingli" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich-Zwingli-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans Asper, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Old Swiss Confederacy showed that peasant and urban communities could defend their liberties against princely power and govern themselves without a king, an example both admired and feared in late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its infantry transformed warfare: the success of Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> against mounted knights helped end the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>, their tactics were copied by the German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknechte</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Swiss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> served in most major European armies for three centuries. The Confederacy was also a cradle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> began reform in Zürich from 1519, and the allied city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a> worked from 1536 and founded his academy in 1559, became the centre of Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, whose ideas spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and the English-speaking world. Basel's printers and scholars made it a hub of humanism. The cantons' avoidance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War and later conflicts laid the foundation for Swiss neutrality, recognised internationally in 1815. In the eighteenth century the image of free, virtuous Alpine republics, shaped by writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-haller">Albrecht von Haller</a> and the Genevan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, influenced European political thought. The Confederacy's institutions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a>, cantonal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and consensus through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a>, remain the historical roots of the federalism and direct democracy of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grauholz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-grauholz.jpg" alt="Battle of Grauholz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grauholz">Battle of Grauholz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GrauholzWalthard_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The original uploader was Weberschiffchen at German Wikipedia., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the eighteenth century the Confederacy was rigid and divided. Power in most cantons had narrowed to small hereditary oligarchies, subject territories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a> had no political rights, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villmergen-wars">Villmergen War</a> of 1712, won by Zürich and Bern, confirmed Reformed dominance without creating common institutions. Unrest in the subject lands and among excluded townspeople was repeatedly suppressed, as in the execution of Major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-davel">Abraham Davel</a> in 1723 after his attempt to free Vaud from Bernese rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> brought the crisis to a head. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-guards">Swiss Guards</a> died defending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuileries-palace">Tuileries</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in August 1792, and revolutionary ideas found supporters in Vaud, Basel and among exiles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-cesar-de-la-harpe">Frédéric-César de La Harpe</a>. In early 1798 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">French Directory</a> sent armies into the Confederacy; Vaud proclaimed itself free, and after the defeat of Bernese forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grauholz">Battle of Grauholz</a> on 5 March 1798, Bern surrendered and the old order collapsed. On 12 April 1798 the centralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> was proclaimed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aarau">Aarau</a>, abolishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the cantons and their subject territories. It proved unstable, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> of 1803 restored a federation of nineteen cantons. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 recognised Swiss neutrality, and after the brief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonderbund-war">Sonderbund War</a> of 1847 the federal constitution of 1848 created the modern Swiss state.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Old Swiss Confederacy, a league of rural and urban communities that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> and Burgundian armies, produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s most feared infantry and the Zwinglian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and its self-governing cantons, consensus-based <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> and habit of neutrality are the direct roots of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>'s federalism and direct democracy.</em></p>

